{
  "dom_id": "question-129625",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "Consider a function $g(x)$ such that $g(1)=\\frac{2}{3}$ and ${g}^{′}(1)=-1.$ If $h(x)=\\frac{1-4f(x)}{g(x)},$ then ${h}^{′}(1)=$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        .",
  "worked_solution": "In part 2 we found that ${f}^{′}(1)=\\frac{5}{8}.$ Also,\n\n$$\nf(1)=\\frac{1+1}{2\\cdot {1}^{2}-11\\cdot 1+5}=-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nThe quotient rule states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{u}{v})=\\frac{{u}^{′}v-u{v}^{′}}{{v}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nLet $u=1-4f(x)$ and $v=g(x).$ Differentiating each, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-4{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{and}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}={g}^{′}(x).\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the quotient rule gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{{u}^{′}v-u{v}^{′}}{{v}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-4{f}^{′}(x)\\cdot g(x)-(1-4f(x))\\cdot {g}^{′}(x)}{(g(x))^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt $x=1,$ this gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{′}(1) & =\\frac{-4{f}^{′}(1)\\cdot g(1)-(1-4f(1))\\cdot {g}^{′}(1)}{(g(1))^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-4\\cdot \\frac{5}{8})\\cdot \\frac{2}{3}-(1-4\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2}))\\cdot (-1)}{{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-\\frac{5}{3}+3)}{(\\frac{4}{9})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{4}{3})}{(\\frac{4}{9})} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
